<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shango</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ṣàngó, Xangô, Changó</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shango is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> of fire, thunder, lightning, drumming, dance, strength and justice in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion. Tradition holds that he was the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaafin">Alaafin</a>, or king, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oranmiyan">Oranmiyan</a> and Ajaka, a warlike ruler whose seven-year reign ended when lightning destroyed his palace, and who was deified after his death; his wives are named as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oshun">Oshun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> and Oya. He is known for his double-headed axe, the oṣé. Enslaved Yoruba carried his worship to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, where as Xangô in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> and Changó in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a> he is identified with Saint Barbara or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Saint Jerome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shango.jpg" alt="Shango" width="330" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dornicke · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Representação_de_Xangô_MN_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> tradition Shango is a royal ancestor as well as a god: as the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaafin">Alaafin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> he is said to have brought the empire prosperity before his deification. According to Professor Mason's Mythological Account of Heroes and Kings, he was, unlike his peaceful brother Ajaka, a powerful, bellicose and violent ruler, whose seven years on the throne were filled with campaigns and battles. A related deity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a>, Ara, whose name means thunder, is often closely associated with him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Two narratives describe him. In one he casts a thunderstone to earth against anyone who offends him, and worshippers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> avoid cowpea for fear of his wrath. In the other he breathes fire and smoke through his nostrils and is angered by his quarrelsome wives. He was feared for tyranny and destructive power but revered for bravery and fairness. His necklaces are of red and white <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, usually in groups of four or six, his sacred numbers, and stones made by lightning are kept at sacred sites and used in rituals.</p><p>He is worshipped on the fifth day of the week, Ojo Jakuta, with the bata drum, red clothing and foods such as guguru, bitter cola, àmàlà and gbegiri soup. Shango is still called on during coronation ceremonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Devotion to him gave its name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> he is Ogou Chango of the Nago nation, and Palo knows him as Siete Rayos. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a>, Xangô is believed to have many manifestations, among them Ayrá, Agodo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonja">Afonja</a>. He gained strong importance among the enslaved in Brazil for his strength, resistance and aggression and became patron of plantations and many terreiros, while Oko, the orixá of agriculture, found little favour. The main barracão of the terreiro Casa Branca is dedicated to him, and his ritual dish, amalá, is served on Wednesday at his altar. He is the protagonist of Duro Ladipo's play Ọba kò so of 1963.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shango” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Every initiate is given a new name, the nome de santo, which usually indicates the identity of their tutelary orixá, and receives a string of beads coloured for that orixá: white for Oxalá, dark blue for Ogum, red and white for <b>Xangô</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/candomble">Candomblé · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Beneath this divinity are the oricha, also called ocha or santos, who usually take their names and attributes from Yoruba deities and are equated with Roman Catholic saints: <b>Changó</b> with Santa Barbara, Babalú Ayé with Saint Lazarus, Ochún with Cuba's patron saint, Our Lady of Charity.</blockquote><a href="/religion/santeria">Santería · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>The Oyo Empire, which from the 17th century dominated much of the Yoruba country and the lands to the west, spread the worship of <b>Shango</b>, the orisha of thunder, remembered in tradition as an early king of Oyo.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
